Apple trackpads: macOS's 0.169 mm position deadband, from the first touch.

macOS has a hysteresis after all, and 0002's premise that it has none was
wrong. HSTouchHIDService (macOS 27.2, the user-space gesture engine for the
built-in trackpad) passes every touch's position through
MTParserPath_::applyAbsolutePixelHysteresis (0x70b6c) before any pointer or
scroll delta is taken: on each axis on its own, the centre stays put until
the finger is more than the margin away from it, then trails the finger by
the margin, and the deltas are the centre's steps. The margin is
MTPathFilterParameters_ +0x38 = 2.66 px of 1/400 in = 0.16891 mm (+0x3c)
for the built-in pad (initPathFilterParams 0x8089c, constant at 0x109088),
and it is always on. A finger therefore has to travel 0.169 mm on an axis
before it moves anything, and a change of direction absorbs twice that.

So instead of the wobble detector switching libinput's elliptical quarter
millimetre on for good at some point, Apple trackpads get macOS's per-axis
0.169 mm deadband from the start (17 units a side at the 100 per mm the
bakery's kernel advertises). Taps measure their movement from the
deadbanded position, as macOS counts tap motion only in emitted counts.

Not ported: during the first 48 ms after touchdown (fading out by 312 ms,
and only until the touch first escapes the band) macOS also drags the
centre along with the finger by up to 0.8 mm per frame, scaled by an
instability measure that includes pressure and size changes
(pullHysteresisCenterHidingUnstablePixelDeltasXY 0x7083c,
measureInstability 0x711a0), so the settling of a landing finger moves
nothing. Nor is the position rounded to whole 1/400 in steps, which only
matters together with macOS's per-event acceleration.

